Driver packs
============

The image has no hardware drivers that only some apps need (sound, GPU,
webcam, USB serial). Each app's install script installs the drivers it
needs, as a "driver pack", into /data/alpine.

How it works
- board/wyse/linux.fragment (both models) and linux-<model>.fragment turn
  the pack drivers on as modules (=m).
  That way the image kernel holds the small shared parts they need.
- post-build.sh keeps only 8 modules in the image (IMAGE_MODULES) and
  sets the rest aside in $BUILD_DIR/wyse-driver-pool.
- drivers/build.sh <model> <pack> takes the modules listed in
  drivers/<model>/<pack>.modules plus every module they need, and writes
  drivers/out/wyse-drivers-<model>-<pack>-<kernel>.tar.gz.
  ./build.sh builds all packs of the model after the image.

Packs (only the 3040 has packs so far)
- sound: Wyse 3040 built-in sound (SOF, rt5672 card) and USB sound cards.
  Firmware comes from Alpine (apk add sof-firmware).
- kvm:   i915 (Intel GPU), uvcvideo (USB capture/webcam), ch341 (USB serial).
  User-space parts come from Alpine (apk add libva libva-intel-driver).
  Used by apps/simple-kvm/install.sh.

A pack only loads into the exact kernel it was built with. After any kernel
change, rebuild the packs and reinstall them on the devices.

On the device (what an install script does)
  alpine tar -xzf - -C / < wyse-drivers-3040-sound-$(uname -r).tar.gz
  alpine depmod $(uname -r)        # apk add kmod first
  printf %s /data/alpine/lib/firmware > /sys/module/firmware_class/parameters/path
  alpine modprobe -a <modules>
Modules are not loaded by themselves at boot: the app's run file loads them.
See apps/audio-player/install.sh and apps/simple-kvm/install.sh.
